WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.960
In my last video I discussed how adding a&nbsp;
single card to an EDH deck that simplifies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.080
or circumvents a task that a player finds fun&nbsp;
can have a negative impact on how playing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.440
deck feels. That video generated a lot of&nbsp;
buzz and discussion, which was great to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.280
and it also helped crystalize a handful of ideas&nbsp;
that have been sloshing around my brain for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.280
past several months. These ideas are complicated&nbsp;
and span a variety of subjects, but they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.840
be pretty straightforwardly introduced with a&nbsp;
simple thought experiment: let’s say you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:36.680
a tutor and there’s a bonkers card, something&nbsp;
very powerful in your deck. Do you tutor for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:41.480
the bonkers card? Just so things are a bit more&nbsp;
tangible, let’s say you’re Enlightened-Tutoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:46.880
for a Skullclamp in a mid-power tokens deck.&nbsp;
A decently powerful tutor, and a draw engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.160
that is probably leaps and bounds better than the&nbsp;
next most powerful draw engine in the deck. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.320
do you Enlightened Tutor for the Skullclamp?&nbsp;
I have devised four answers to this question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:02.920
which segment four different perspectives&nbsp;
on EDH philosophy, and those go as follows:

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.440
Yes, I tutor for the Skullclamp&nbsp;
card because I like Skullclamp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.040
and I want to draw a shitload of&nbsp;
cards, regardless of situation.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.520
No, I might choose not to tutor for the Skullclamp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.280
because the number of cards it draws&nbsp;
might not fit the vibe of this game.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.880
No, and in fact I cut the Enlightened Tutor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.240
because I don’t want the option&nbsp;
of tutoring for the Skullclamp.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.440
No, and in fact I cut the&nbsp;
Skullclamp because the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:28.000
of cards it draws doesn’t fit the vibe of my deck.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.200
It may be clear which of these quadrants that I&nbsp;
personally fall into most frequently—the fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.720
one, I’ve found, no hate to Skullclamp in&nbsp;
particular—but I want to acknowledge that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:42.560
other three exist. I’ve gotten a lot of comments&nbsp;
from people who likely fall into one of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.120
three quadrants, people saying something to the&nbsp;
effect of “but wait Alex, have you considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.240
this? I think you forgot to consider this.” So,&nbsp;
I now want to consider the other three of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:58.000
perspectives in depth and where they come from,&nbsp;
what core animating forces drive people to engage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.200
with this format the way they do. I’ve split&nbsp;
this video up into two parts, because I found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.800
that the first two perspectives here and the&nbsp;
last two perspectives here really demand two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.560
different lenses of analysis. So, if you want to&nbsp;
hear me discuss the final two you can keep an eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.920
out for that video about a week from now, but&nbsp;
for now let’s get into the first two quadrants.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.440
The first one is the person who does tutor&nbsp;
for the good card because they want to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.680
the good card. This is, on its face, entirely&nbsp;
understandable. Magic: the Gathering is a card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:33.560
game where players try to win games, and winning&nbsp;
games is fun. More powerful cards win more games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:39.120
so they end up being seen as especially fun&nbsp;
in some sense. This may be directly related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.720
to the fact that they win more games,&nbsp;
but in a format like EDH it could also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.440
be a more indirect force—they feel good to&nbsp;
play, they generate reactions from others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:53.400
they have a mystique about them borne from&nbsp;
years of being heralded as powerful. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.320
a reason why vintage cube is one of the&nbsp;
most popular cube formats on MTGO—are these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.040
powerful cards more inherently fun than any&nbsp;
others? No, but they’re famous and alluring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:09.080
and playing them pokes a little good card dopamine&nbsp;
button residing in the brains of a lot of players.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.800
The EDH player who runs good cards and tutors to&nbsp;
find those good cards more often may be doing so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.360
because they want to win more games, but they&nbsp;
also may just be looking to poke that button&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:23.440
more. And can you blame them? It’s fun to play a&nbsp;
Skullclamp and draw a ludicrous number of cards.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.600
I think most players go through a stage of this at&nbsp;
some point in time—I certainly had the experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:33.520
of optimizing a mono black pile with Cabal&nbsp;
Coffers and Necropotence and such like ten or so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.000
years ago, and I remember the excitement I felt.&nbsp;
For some players, that excitement does persist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.600
and I’m happy for those players—maybe they make&nbsp;
their way to cEDH, or maybe they just have a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:49.080
time playing sweaty high-power piles against other&nbsp;
sweaty high-power piles. However, for myself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.480
many other players, the excitement of playing&nbsp;
good cards for the sake of them being good does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:59.720
start to fade with time. Trotting out the same old&nbsp;
powerful cards just might not remain as enjoyable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.160
and even if you do keep playing with them you&nbsp;
might need to change the way you play with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:09.760
them to keep them fresh. One way this can be&nbsp;
done is changing the context in which you play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.160
a powerful card. An Ancient Tomb that curves&nbsp;
into a Bolas’s Citadel is very different from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:20.160
one that curves into a bunch of treefolk, and a&nbsp;
Necropotence that draws into an infinite combo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:25.960
is very different from one that draws a bunch of&nbsp;
Clerics from the 2000s. Put another way, powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:31.480
cards can go from a fun thing in their own right&nbsp;
to a way to enable some other fun things that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:37.560
a bit less powerful. This isn’t really a realm of&nbsp;
deckbuilding I tend to find myself in, but I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:43.240
absolutely see the appeal—you get the enjoyment&nbsp;
of playing powerful Magic cards and the enjoyment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:48.720
of enabling offbeat archetypes that might not be&nbsp;
good enough with weaker support cards. And, going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:54.120
back to the premise of this video, tutors can help&nbsp;
with this. If you want to guarantee having a piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.640
of powerful support to hold up your otherwise&nbsp;
questionable gameplan, tutors can be a good way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:05.080
do that, to have a consistent experience in your&nbsp;
janky deck. The game doesn’t have 10 Necropotences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:10.320
you can slip into your deck, and even if it did&nbsp;
that might not even be desirable. Tutors are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:16.440
nice way to work around that fact. Okay, so that’s&nbsp;
one way powerful cards can stay fresh—playing them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.760
in unusual contexts. The other way is getting&nbsp;
to the point where you don’t even feel the need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.200
to play the good card every time it’s available,&nbsp;
and this brings me to the second group of players.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.280
These are the players who have the tutor&nbsp;
and don’t tutor for the Skullclamp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:36.400
or at least they don’t always tutor for the best&nbsp;
possible option. I’ve found that these are players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.480
who see tutors as a wonderful and freeing tool—you&nbsp;
can find whatever you want with them! You can find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:48.080
a Skullclamp, you can find a land, you can find&nbsp;
a Leeching Licid—the world is your oyster. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.960
there is a certain logic to this. Some games&nbsp;
you might want a power-card—maybe you’re behind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.280
or maybe you want a chance at catching up with&nbsp;
somebody running away with the game—but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.800
also might be other games when you’d prefer to&nbsp;
take it a little easier, play a bit lower caliber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.000
tutor for something a bit more mundane&nbsp;
if it better fits the vibe of the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:10.560
you’re playing. I believe this to be the least&nbsp;
popular of the four quadrants I laid out earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.720
but for the people who fall into it, I’m&nbsp;
genuinely happy for them. They are able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:20.960
to have fun doing something that I struggle to&nbsp;
have fun doing—playing suboptimally on purpose.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:25.880
See, I treat the format of EDH as a two-stage&nbsp;
game, as I discussed in more detail in a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:30.920
about combo decks last fall. During the&nbsp;
first stage, the deckbuilding stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:35.560
I’m in creative mode, crafting an experience for&nbsp;
the future me to play with, thinking about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:39.800
sorts of decks I might be facing and trying to&nbsp;
fine-tune what I’m building to create the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:44.840
enjoyable games. During the second stage&nbsp;
though, when I’m actually playing a game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:50.760
my goals are somewhat different. I treat it as a&nbsp;
game where my objective is winning, and where I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:56.160
pursuing that under a set of constraints. These&nbsp;
constraints consist of the tools given to me by my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:00.600
deckbuilding self, as well as the limitations&nbsp;
of the type of table engagement I prefer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:05.840
which is generally very open and disclosing of&nbsp;
information I think my opponents should have. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:11.120
not doggedly pursuing victory at all costs, but I&nbsp;
also don’t like playing suboptimally once I’m in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:16.440
a game—if I need to make a suboptimal choice or&nbsp;
hold back in some way, it pierces the immersion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:21.440
of the game and kinda takes me out of it. I&nbsp;
still will do this under some circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.640
but I don’t like doing it and I certainly wouldn’t&nbsp;
set up my deck in a way where I expect to need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.960
to do it on a regular basis. I know I’m not&nbsp;
alone in this either—a lot of people dislike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:36.960
playing suboptimally, and another segment of&nbsp;
players dislike playing against somebody they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.880
know is playing suboptimally. It almost feels&nbsp;
like an insult—you could interpret somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:45.720
choosing to play make worse choices than they&nbsp;
possibly could as them saying “oh no, I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:50.880
need to put full effort into this game, what&nbsp;
you’re doing there isn’t requiring that of me.”

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:55.200
Now realistically, the sort of player who is&nbsp;
okay with playing suboptimally probably isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.120
thinking this way, but for somebody who is in&nbsp;
a somewhat more competitive mindset it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.040
hard to take this interpretation. This type&nbsp;
of player may also seem very different from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:09.120
most other ways of playing in the EDH format,&nbsp;
and indeed, it does take a different mindset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.560
a different perspective on the nature of&nbsp;
winning a game, maybe even different ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:18.040
of what a game should look like. However,&nbsp;
going back to the two-phase idea of Magic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:23.200
the Gathering, this difference could be as simple&nbsp;
as reversing the ordering of optimization in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:29.640
terms of deckbuilding vs playing. I play in a more&nbsp;
optimized style and deckbuild in a less optimized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:33.840
style, but it’s easy to imagine a type of player&nbsp;
who puts more optimization effort into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:40.000
deckbuilding phase and is a bit more laid back and&nbsp;
casual when they’re actually playing. I might feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.800
a little weird about somebody else making choices&nbsp;
that notably worsen their chances of winning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:49.120
but I’ve also had people complain about me making&nbsp;
the same sorts of choices in the deckbuilding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:55.920
stage of things. The most dominant feature of&nbsp;
casual EDH is some degree of suboptimality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:59.000
and I generally think of that as being&nbsp;
in the deckbuilding part of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:03.760
but it seems reasonable enough that it&nbsp;
could exist in the playing stage instead.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:07.520
As a final note, going back to the&nbsp;
comparison between these two player types,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:12.440
a way these two players are a bit more similar&nbsp;
is in the character of their decks. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:16.200
generally both happy to play tutors, and they&nbsp;
are both happy to play with powerful cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.520
maybe even when those cards might feel&nbsp;
a bit overtuned compared to the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:25.560
the contents of a deck. Fundamentally, these&nbsp;
things are just gamepieces, and having them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.560
a deck isn’t an obstacle to the fun for these&nbsp;
people. What I’ll be talking about next week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:35.360
on the other hand, is the opposite—it’s players&nbsp;
who are much more critical of what they put in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:40.600
their deck, in one way or another. And,&nbsp;
as you’ll see when that video comes out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:46.280
those particular perspectives have philosophical&nbsp;
roots about as deep as it’s possible to be.

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:50.920
With that said, thanks for watching! And thanks&nbsp;
to Archidekt for supporting the channel. I’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.680
doing a lot of brewing lately, and Archidekt makes&nbsp;
it easy to keep my thoughts organized with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:02.920
nice visual layout and helpful category system. A&nbsp;
link to my decks is in the description. I’d also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:07.160
like to thank my Patrons, some of whom are on&nbsp;
screen now. I just started taking submissions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:11.600
for this month’s deck review, so if you want&nbsp;
a chance at having your deck reviewed by me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:15.400
you can sign up to my Patreon at the link&nbsp;
in the description. You can also check out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:19.120
the podcast I’m on if you want to experience&nbsp;
more words exiting my mouth without giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:24.320
me more money. A link to that is also&nbsp;
in the description. Alright, bye now.

